      "content": "When a woodwind instrument produces a note, the vibration causes the air column inside the reed or cavity to produce a fundamental frequency, as well as higher-order harmonics or overtones. The unique shape and size of the instrument, as well as the material of the reed or air column, determine which overtones are emphasized and to what degree. The oboe, for example, has a relatively narrow air column, which produces a prominent,F fundamental sense-caffe-withaúmeros_OF controlId overs contribute significantly to its characteristic sound, making it sound brighter and more piercing than the flute, which has a longer air column and emphasis on lower overtones. This interplay between fundamental and overtones affects the overall timbre (tone color) of the instrument, giving each distinct character.",
